  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nHEAVY RAIN TODAY...It's quite the soggy start to Friday across west-\ncentral and southern MN, where MPX radar imagery captures an \nexpansive shield of moderate to heavy rainfall. Driving the \nprecipitation is a stacked upper-wave that is progged to mature\ninto a closed upper-low as it digs southeast from the Dakotas \ninto Iowa today. At the surface, cyclogenesis is underway across\neastern South Dakota and guidance depicts the low sliding to \nthe southeast through central Iowa by midday. Such an evolution \nplaces the western & southwestern portions of MN in a zone \nfavored for a one-two punch of warm advective precipitation (as \nwe're seeing early this morning) and a heavy deformation band of\nrainfall that will develop on the northern side of the mid- \nlevel low and prolong the heavy rain threat through the morning.\nCombine the synoptic forcing with an environment primed for \nefficient rainfall owing to skinny CAPE, a very deep warm cloud\nlayer, rich PWATs (2+\"), and slow storm motions and the pieces \nare in place for a flood threat. The overall theme in the 00z \nCAM guidance supports the going forecast, with a wide swath of \n2-4\" of rain along and near the MN River and a narrow, more \nlocalized swath of amounts exceeding 4\" -- it's very possible a \nfew locations measure upwards of 6\" for a storm total! With this\nin mind, our eyes are on the flash flood indicators early this \nmorning. 2 AM MRMS data illustrates that locations across Chippewa\nCounty, MN have likely already observed 3-4\" with rain ongoing.\nOur Flash Flood Watch remains in effect for all counties south \nof a Swift to Meeker line. The majority of the Watch will expire\nat noon, however we've followed timing trends in the CAMs and \nopted to extend the Watch until 4 PM for the southern two tiers \nof counties along I-90. As noted in WPC MPD #901, dry antecedent\nsoils should work to mitigate the overall flood threat, however\nthe long duration nature of the moderate to at times heavy rain\ncertainly bears watching.\n\nFor those in the Twin Cities Metro, the forecast remains largely \nunchanged, as a sharp cut-off in rainfall amounts is expected from \nthe southwest portion of the Metro (Carver/Scott) to the northeast \n(Anoka/Washington). We've observed a slight northerly shift in some \nof the latest CAM guidance (the last several runs of the HRRR in \nparticular). Should these solutions come to fruition, it's possible \nthat more of the Metro could observe amounts closer to or exceeding \nan inch, but again these chances would favor communities to the \nsouth and west of the immediate Twin Cities Metro. Rain will end \nfrom northwest to southeast as the surface low departs towards \neastern Iowa this afternoon. One other note, we have boosted \nwinds across southern MN this afternoon as near term guidance \nis more aggressive featuring 35-40 mph gusts in response to the\nstrengthening system.\n\nNICE WEEKEND WEATHER...By Saturday, the well-defined closed upper-\nlow is forecast to take residence over the Ohio Valley. Ridging will \nexpand east across the northern CONUS, which supports a quiet weekend\nforecast for the Upper Midwest. Forecast highs are in the upper 70s\nto lower 80s Saturday & Sunday, with the anticipation of mostly\nsunny skies overhead. It's possible that the skies may appear \nhazy at times due to wildfire smoke present in the upper-levels \nof the atmosphere, however impactful surface smoke is not \nanticipated at this time.\n\nSTORM CHANCE EARLY NEXT WEEK...A progressive shortwave supported by \nan active Pacific jet is forecast to traverse through central \nCanada early next week. The majority of the upper-level forcing \nassociated with this feature will slide north of the Upper \nMidwest, however a trailing cold front is forecast to move \nacross the Dakotas into MN later Monday into Tuesday. Early \nindications are that moisture return ahead of the front may be \nlacking, so it's not surprising to see NBM only feature 40-60 \nPoPs across western MN that lower to 20-30 PoPs in eastern MN \nlate Monday night. Of note, several of the AI severe weather \nforecast tools highlight northwestern MN (where NBM's PoPs are \nhigher) for a chance of stronger storms, which makes sense given\nproximity to the upper-forcing. We'll keep an eye on this \nthrough the weekend. The front is forecast to sag south Tuesday \nand may force new convection across southern MN in the afternoon.\nDrier weather returns by way of high pressure for the middle of\nnext week.",
